WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:06.690
Since 2005, millions have tuned in to CBS
to catch the Behavioral Analysis Unit track

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:09.190
down "unsubs" on Criminal Minds.

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:14.710
A show with that kind of lifespan generates
a lot of on-set stories and trivia, much of

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:16.670
it well-known to casual fans.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:20.530
But there are some tidbits only the show's
diehard fans know about.

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:25.210
Here are five facts you might not know about
the mega-popular procedural.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:26.210
In the line of duty

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:31.580
Early in the fifth season of Criminal Minds,
Spencer Reid, played by Matthew Gray Gubler,

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:34.039
is shot in leg in the line of duty.

00:00:34.039 --> 00:00:38.489
For the next several unrelated episodes, Reid
is shown walking with crutches.

00:00:38.489 --> 00:00:43.190
It's a realistic touch - and dramatically
effective, showing that sometimes the good

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:47.790
guys suffer for being the good guys - but
the shooting and the crutches actually had

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.859
to be written into the episode.

00:00:49.859 --> 00:00:56.159
Just before season five started filming in
2009, Gubler dislocated his knee… from dancing

00:00:56.159 --> 00:00:57.800
too hard.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.859
Unscripted sweetness

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:04.800
Along with all the murder, assault, kidnappings,
and other darkness that fuels the Criminal

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:10.440
Minds plot machine is a lighthearted palate-cleanser:
the wordy flirtations between Shemar Moore's

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.350
Derek Morgan and Kirsten Vangsness Penelope
Garcia.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:16.960
"Oh you are the light of my life, sweet lady."

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:18.680
[Speaking French]

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.900
"Drives me crazy when you talk that voulez-coucher
stuff to me.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:22.900
Stop it!"

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:28.310
Until Moore left the series in 2016, the two
frequently called each other cute and affectionate

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:32.280
names; Morgan was particularly fond of calling
Garcia "baby girl."

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:37.200
It feels spontaneous and organic, because
it is - those exchanges were based on a bit

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.560
of improvisation on Moore's part while shooting
a first-season episode.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:46.340
Fans loved it, but Moore kept it under control:
He said he set a limit of no more than one

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.380
or two "baby girls" per episode.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.570
No Love for Jennifer

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:55.730
After years as a star of teen-oriented movies
and TV shows, Jennifer Love Hewitt settled

00:01:55.730 --> 00:02:00.220
into a career as reliable, familiar presence
on primetime TV dramas.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:06.420
In 2014 she was cast as investigator Kate
Callahan on Criminal Minds, ostensibly replacing

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:09.570
the departing Jeanne Tripplehorn and her character,
Alex Blake.

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:13.300
"It's such a different job for me than I've
ever had before, so it's great!"

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:17.720
Some fans did not take to the former Party
of Five star, nor her character.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:22.410
In addition to the customary negative social
media chatter, a small number of particularly

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:28.330
upset fans even signed a petition at Change.org
to "Write Jennifer Love Hewitt's character

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:30.120
off Criminal Minds."

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:34.510
One fan wrote that "developing a new character
just takes away screen time and distracts

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:39.530
from the cases" and "the acting and writing
for this character is just wild corny."

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:43.780
Hewitt left the show after Season 10, citing
her pregnancy.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:44.990
No reality show

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:50.940
The Behavioral Analysis Unit is a real division
of the FBI that's really based in Quantico,

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:57.110
Virginia, and it's very selective: only 26
of the FBI's 12,000 agents are part of the

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:58.110
department.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:02.370
But life in the BAU isn't quite like it's
depicted on Criminal Minds.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:07.540
Only a handful of the most complicated and
extreme cases ever make their way to the small,

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:12.940
elite real-life BAU team, and they certainly
don't solve a case per week.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:16.950
And unlike on TV, it's almost entirely a desk
job.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:20.650
Rarely do BAU team members leave Quantico
to hit the field.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:24.370
When they do leave, they're certainly not
the ones who confront criminals and put their

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:29.180
lives in danger while doing so - the vast
majority of that action is handled by police

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:31.800
and other law enforcement officials.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.099
In her own words

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:38.560
The set of Criminal Minds seems to foster
a collaborative, creatively inclusive vibe.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:43.300
Numerous cast members past and present have
directed episodes of the series.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:49.290
Kirsten Vangsness has also co-written three
installments of Criminal Minds since 2015.

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:52.750
It's hardly a lark, as Vangsness is an accomplished
playwright.

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:57.570
In fact, Vangsness says the writers expect
her to rewrite the many phone call conversations

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.220
required of her character.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:04.290
They also asked her to write the final scenes
between her character and Derek Morgan when

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:07.230
Shemar Moore left Criminal Minds in 2016.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:11.580
"You will always be my original baby girl."

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:12.690
Thanks for watching!

